<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened runtime entitlements for the Phosphor desktop shell.

  WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR. The keystore encrypts the keys at rest. That moves them off disk and
  into the heap of a running process, and on macOS a same-user process can attach to another
  with task_for_pid and read that heap. Encryption at rest without a hardened runtime therefore
  moves the key from a file anyone can read to a heap anyone can read, which is not a control.
  The two ship together or neither is real.

  THE ENTITLEMENT THAT IS NOT HERE IS THE POINT. `com.apple.security.get-task-allow` is absent,
  and absent is what matters: it is the entitlement that lets a debugger attach to this process.
  Do not add it, not even temporarily, and not for a "debug build" that might get shipped. The
  three false values below are explicit denials of things the hardened runtime would otherwise
  let this process ask for, written out rather than omitted so that adding one is a visible edit.

  OPEN QUESTION, for whoever runs the first signed build.
  `com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation` is the one key here that WEAKENS the
  runtime: it allows dylibs signed by anyone to be loaded into the process, which is close to the
  thing the hardened runtime exists to prevent. It is here because the bundle ships its own node
  binary in `externalBin`, and that is the usual reason given for it.

  It may well be unnecessary. Library validation governs dylibs loaded INTO a process, and
  `tauri build` signs the sidecar with the same identity as the app, so a same-team sidecar
  satisfies library validation without this. The payload's three dependencies (viem, zod and the
  MCP SDK) are pure JavaScript with no native addons, so node loads no third-party dylibs either.

  That cannot be settled without a real signed build, and only the owner holds the identity. So:
  at the first signed build, set this to <false/>, build, and run the app. If it launches and the
  backend spawns, delete this key and this paragraph. If it does not, keep it and record the
  failure here so the next person does not repeat the experiment.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <false/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <false/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.debugger</key>
  <false/>
</dict>
</plist>
